    description: "FOUND 2026-09-28 while fixing INV-vusum (the typed-receiver half, which puts the receiver's TYPE in Go's module slot and prefers the exactly-named row).\n\nTHE SHAPE LEFT OVER. A method called directly on a package function's result, `pkg.Func(args).Method()`, goes through go.py's chained-call branch, which knows only the inner call's PACKAGE and sets the slot to it. So the method is still credited to a same-named package FUNCTION row by declaration order:\n    slog.New(h).Error(msg)          -> log/slog.Error          (should be log/slog.Logger.Error)\n    json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(v)    -> slot encoding/json      (the type is json.Encoder)\nMeasured on prometheus (scrape/scrape.go scrapeLoop.scrapeAndReport: `slog.New(sl.scrapeFailureLogger).Error(...)`). Every boundary is unchanged by the mislabel today (both rows of each function/method pair carry one boundary), so it is a LABEL defect, as the 17 of 18 INV-vusum pairs were.\n\nWHAT IS NOT KNOWN HERE: the return type of an external package function. _type_from_constructor_call infers `pkg.NewFoo()` -> `pkg.Foo` by the NewXxx convention, which covers json.NewEncoder but not slog.New. library_signatures/go.yaml already holds package_variables types; a return-type table for the stdlib constructors (slog.New, http.NewRequest, bufio.NewReader ...) would be the matching home.\n\nFIRST STEP: route _type_from_constructor_call's `pkg.Foo` into the chained-call branch through _external_package_for_type (the same owner the typed-local branch now builds), then measure what a small return-type table adds on alertmanager / prometheus."  # 32f7
    fields: {}  # 32f7
- op: discuss  # c655
